Implementation:

    Date.cpp

        I stored the month, day and year as integers so that I could store strings 
        of the different month names in a vector and access them using vector[month-1]
        in the print_date() function. In the constructor I used a series of while loops 
        to iterate through the different parameters within the input date and append each 
        number that wasn't a leading 0 to a string representing the month, day or year.  
        Then I used the built in stoi() function to change the strings to integers and
        input initialize the date object.

    Person.cpp

        Constructor(filename)
            Read in person using getline() for fname, lname, date, then used series of while
            loops to read in type and phone number, as well as type and email number from the
            file similar to the implementation from date.cpp.

        Constructor(string arguments)
            For this constructor, I essentially did the same thing as the other one except I
            prompt the user for the necessary information.

    Network.cpp

        loadDB()
            First I use a DLL destructor (similar to lab 9) to clear the current network.
            Then I read in each line and call the person constructor, in a loop and skip
            the dashed line using getline() and push_front() to add each person to the DLL.

        saveDB()
            In this function, I open the desired file and print the each element of the DLL 
            in the specified format, followed by a dashed line.

        push_back()
            In this function, I set the new element->next to null, then new element->prev to
            tail.  Then I update the tail to the new element and the count to count + 1.

        search() ? 
            TA said to ignore, just implement search(firstname, lastname) and 
            search(Person* searchEntry).

        remove(fname, lname)
            I use search(fname, lname) to find the person if it exists, and if does,
            I remove it from the list by re-linking the DLL around the person and 
            deleting the person.

        destructor
            I iterate through the DLL, setting a temporary pointer to the head, moving
            the head to head->next, then deleting the temporary pointer.

        search(Person* searchEntry)
            Explained in notes.

        search(fname, lname)
            I iterate through the list and compare the arguments to each person's name,
            returning a pointer to NULL if none of them match, or a pointer to the first
            matching person.

        Unconventional implemenations for options:

            option 2:
                listFiles() is a function that validates the format of each text file in
                the available directory by checking if the total number of lines/6 is equal
                to the number of dashed lines.  Then it prints the names of these files.

            option 3:
                For this option, I just used call remove, then push_front so that if the Person
                exists, they get removed and readded, and if they don't they get added.  This 
                does change the order of the list, but that doesn't affect the system.  I did not
                do this because it was optimal in terms of time complexity, I did it because it
                was easy.

